WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:06.810
So, over the last 23 years we've been
studying the center of the galaxy with

00:00:06.810 --> 00:00:11.429
the largest telescopes in the world — that's the Keck telescope — and by developing new ways

00:00:11.429 --> 00:00:15.719
of using this telescope to get to the
diffraction limit we've been able to

00:00:15.719 --> 00:00:20.070
open up a new way of studying
supermassive black holes. That's by

00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:25.920
watching the orbits of stars as they
orbit the center of our galaxy, the Milky

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:28.540
Way. These orbits have been able to show that

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:32.610
there is a supermassive black hole at the center of our galaxy. This is the best

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:35.940
evidence to date that supermassive
black holes exist not only in the center

00:00:35.940 --> 00:00:38.270
of our own galaxy but anywhere in the
universe

00:00:38.270 --> 00:00:45.210
and by continuing to measure the
these orbits — both time and technology helps — we

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:49.350
have the ability to explore the physics
of supermassive black holes.

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:54.750
In other words: Testing how black holes
warp space-time as well as the astrophysics

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:59.520
of supermassive black holes — what role
these objects play in the formation and

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:04.799
evolution of galaxies. This is the only galaxy in
which we can watch this interaction that

00:01:04.799 --> 00:01:10.549
we think is key in the evolution of the
universe at play

